First Author: Zafar
Title: Lung Transplantation Advanced Prediction Tool: Determining Recipient’s Outcome for a Certain Donor
Source number: 56  ￼

Very short summary: This UNOS-based study developed a recipient-donor matching risk model and web-based prediction tool for long-term survival after double lung transplantation. Five key findings were: (1) 15,124 double lung transplants in recipients aged ≥12 years were analyzed; (2) the cohort was split into a derivation set of 10,660 and a validation set of 4,464; (3) the final Cox-Lasso model retained 19 variables, including 13 recipient, 4 donor, and 2 transplant factors; (4) the model stratified matches into low-, medium-, and high-risk clusters with similar observed and predicted survival in validation; and (5) the reported C statistics for 1-, 5-, and 10-year survival were 0.67, 0.64, and 0.72, respectively, with estimated half-lives of 10.5, 7.3, and 5.2 years across the three risk groups. Figures 1–7 and Tables 1–3 were used directly for this extraction.  ￼

