	•	Primary ML Component: Yes
	•	Study Design: Retrospective Cohort
	•	AI Model Architecture: Other
	•	Algorithm Name: Cox-Lasso regression; comparison with Cox regression with backward elimination and random forest Cox; Lung Transplantation Advanced Prediction Tool (LAPT)
	•	Input Variables (Data Modality): Tabular (EMR); Donor metrics
	•	Comparator / Standard of Care: None
	•	Validation Method: Internal Split
	•	Explainability / Interpretability Used: No
	•	Feature Selection Method: Automated
	•	Hyperparameter Tuning Reported: Yes
	•	Section 3 Comments/Quotes: The main predictive model was Cox-Lasso, compared with backward-elimination Cox and random-forest Cox. Inputs included recipient, donor, and transplant characteristics from UNOS. The cohort was randomly split into derivation and validation sets, so Internal Split fits best. Variable selection was automated using the Lasso method, and the optimal lambda was selected to balance overfitting and simplicity. I did not see SHAP/LIME-type interpretability; interpretability came through the explicit risk score and cluster assignment.  ￼

Section 4: AI Quality & Reproducibility (CONVINCE Standards)
	•	Missing Data Handling: Multiple Imputation
	•	Class Imbalance Addressed: No
	•	Code Availability: Other
	•	Data Preprocessing / Normalization Described: Yes
	•	Training Size (N): 10660
	•	Test Size (N): 4464
	•	Section 4 Comments/Quotes: The paper states that records with a single missing point were imputed and that “imputation of missing data for recipient and donor variables was conducted separately,” which best fits multiple imputation among the available categories. Preprocessing included exclusions, categorization of continuous variables, expectation-maximization clustering, and derivation/validation splitting. The derivation and validation cohorts were 10,660 and 4,464. I did not find a class-imbalance method. A web-based tool was released, but no public code repository was mentioned, so code availability is best classified as Other.  ￼

